## Experiment 2: Merge Sort Using Divide and Conquer

**Q1. What is Merge Sort?**

Ans: Merge Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses the Divide and Conquer technique.

**Q2. What is Divide and Conquer?**

Ans: An algorithm design technique that divides a problem into smaller subproblems, solves them and combines their results.

**Q3. What are the three steps of Divide and Conquer?**

Ans: Divide, Conquer and Combine.

**Q4. What is the best-case time complexity of Merge Sort?**

Ans: O(n log n) for the standard implementation.

**Q5. What is the average-case time complexity of Merge Sort?**

Ans: O(n log n).

**Q6. What is the worst-case time complexity of Merge Sort?**

Ans: O(n log n).

**Q7. What is the auxiliary space complexity of Merge Sort?**

Ans: O(n) for the standard array-based implementation.

**Q8. Is Merge Sort a stable sorting algorithm?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q9. What is a stable sorting algorithm?**

Ans: A sorting algorithm that preserves the relative order of elements having equal keys.

**Q10. Is standard array-based Merge Sort an in-place sorting algorithm?**

Ans: No, because it requires additional memory for merging.

**Q11. What is recursion?**

Ans: A programming technique in which a function calls itself to solve smaller instances of a problem.

**Q12. What is the base condition of Merge Sort?**

Ans: Recursion stops when the subarray contains zero or one element.

**Q13. What is the recurrence relation of Merge Sort?**

Ans: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n).

**Q14. What is the purpose of the merge() function?**

Ans: To combine two sorted subarrays into a single sorted array.

**Q15. What is the time complexity of merging two sorted subarrays?**

Ans: O(n), where n is the total number of elements in both subarrays.

**Q16. How is the middle index calculated?**

Ans: mid = left + (right - left) / 2.

**Q17. Why is a temporary array used in Merge Sort?**

Ans: To store merged elements before copying them back into the original array.

**Q18. Can Merge Sort handle duplicate elements?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q19. Can Merge Sort handle negative numbers?**

Ans: Yes.

**Q20. What is the main advantage of Merge Sort?**

Ans: It guarantees O(n log n) time complexity and provides stable sorting.

**Q21. What is the main disadvantage of Merge Sort?**

Ans: It requires additional memory for merging.

**Q22. What is the difference between Merge Sort and Quick Sort?**

Ans: Merge Sort divides the array into halves and merges them, whereas Quick Sort partitions elements around a pivot.

**Q23. What is the worst-case time complexity of Quick Sort?**

Ans: O(n squared).

**Q24. Which sorting algorithm is suitable for external sorting?**

Ans: Merge Sort.

**Q25. What is the recursion depth of Merge Sort?**

Ans: O(log n).

**Q26. Why is Merge Sort faster than Bubble Sort for large datasets?**

Ans: Merge Sort has O(n log n) time complexity, whereas Bubble Sort has O(n squared) average and worst-case time complexity.

**Q27. Does Merge Sort require a pivot element?**

Ans: No. Quick Sort uses a pivot element.

**Q28. How can Merge Sort be modified to sort in descending order?**

Ans: Reverse the comparison condition during the merging operation.

**Q29. Why does the merge function use the less-than-or-equal comparison?**

Ans: To select elements from the left subarray first when values are equal, preserving stability.

**Q30. What are the applications of Merge Sort?**

Ans: External sorting, linked list sorting, inversion counting and large dataset processing.
